00:00:11,861 --> 00:00:11,901
¶¶

2
00:00:38,494 --> 00:00:39,335
so

3
00:00:40,495 --> 00:00:40,675
so

4
00:01:19,769 --> 00:01:23,310
When future scholars take a look back at the intellectual history of the last quarter of the

5
00:01:23,330 --> 00:01:24,050
20th century,

6
00:01:24,771 --> 00:01:29,692
the work of Professor Edward Said of Columbia University will be identified as very important

7
00:01:29,752 --> 00:01:30,352
and influential.

8
00:01:31,827 --> 00:01:37,611
In particular, Said's 1978 book, Orientalism, will be regarded as profoundly significant.

9
00:01:39,051 --> 00:01:43,954
Orientalism revolutionized the study of the Middle East and helped to create and shape entire

10
00:01:44,115 --> 00:01:49,438
new fields of study, such as post-colonial theory, as well as influencing disciplines as diverse

11
00:01:49,918 --> 00:01:54,521
as English, history, anthropology, political science, and cultural studies.

12
00:01:56,100 --> 00:02:01,503
The book has now been translated into 26 languages and is required reading at many universities

13
00:02:01,543 --> 00:02:02,024
and colleges.

14
00:02:03,285 --> 00:02:08,388
It is also one of the most controversial scholarly books of the last 30 years, sparking intense

15
00:02:08,408 --> 00:02:09,408
debate and disagreement.

16
00:02:11,089 --> 00:02:15,512
Orientalism tries to answer the question of why, when we think of the Middle East, for example,

17
00:02:16,093 --> 00:02:20,235
we have a preconceived notion of what kind of people live there, what they believe, how they

18
00:02:20,355 --> 00:02:20,535
act,

19
00:02:21,256 --> 00:02:24,598
even though we may never have been there or indeed even met anyone from there.

20
00:02:26,208 --> 00:02:31,632
More generally, Orientalism asks, how do we come to understand people, strangers, who look

21
00:02:31,652 --> 00:02:34,374
different to us by virtue of the color of their skin?

22
00:02:35,996 --> 00:02:40,679
The central argument of Orientalism is that the way we acquire this knowledge is not innocent

23
00:02:40,759 --> 00:02:45,183
or objective, but the end result of a process that reflects certain interests.

24
00:02:46,143 --> 00:02:48,005
That is, it is highly motivated.

25
00:02:49,350 --> 00:02:51,731
Specifically, Said argues that the way the West,

26
00:02:52,491 --> 00:02:57,174
Europe and the US, looks at the countries and peoples of the Middle East is through a lens

27
00:02:57,234 --> 00:03:00,655
that distorts the actual reality of those places and those people.

28
00:03:02,016 --> 00:03:05,837
He calls this lens, through which we view that part of the world, Orientalism,

29
00:03:06,958 --> 00:03:11,380
a framework that we use to understand the unfamiliar and the strange, to make the peoples of

30
00:03:11,400 --> 00:03:13,401
the Middle East appear different and threatening.

31
00:03:15,463 --> 00:03:20,366
Professor Said's contribution to how we understand this general process of what we could call

32
00:03:20,386 --> 00:03:22,528
stereotyping has been immense.

33
00:03:23,888 --> 00:03:27,711
The aim of this program is to explore these issues through an interview with him.

34
00:03:29,212 --> 00:03:33,815
He starts by discussing the context within which he conceived Orientalism.

35
00:03:34,515 --> 00:03:36,237
Well, my interest in Orientalism began

36
00:03:38,943 --> 00:03:45,027
for two reasons. One was an immediate thing, that is to say, the Arab-Israeli War of 1973,

37
00:03:47,150 --> 00:03:52,051
which had been preceded by a lot of images and discussions in the media and the popular press

38
00:03:52,371 --> 00:03:57,472
about how the Arabs are cowardly and they don't know how to fight and they're always going

39
00:03:57,492 --> 00:03:59,172
to be beaten because they're not modern.

40
00:03:59,773 --> 00:04:04,353
And then everybody was very surprised when the Egyptian army crossed the canal in early October

41
00:04:04,373 --> 00:04:08,734
of 1973 and demonstrated that, like anybody else, they could fight.

42
00:04:10,475 --> 00:04:12,015
So that was one immediate.

43
00:04:14,642 --> 00:04:20,429
And the second one, which has a much longer history in my own life, was the constant sort of

44
00:04:20,469 --> 00:04:24,253
disparity I felt between what my experience of being an Arab was

45
00:04:25,215 --> 00:04:28,078
and the representations of that that one saw in art.

46
00:04:28,979 --> 00:04:32,704
I mean, I'm talking about very great artists, you know, like Delacroix and...

47
00:04:33,693 --> 00:04:37,356
Ingres and Jerome and people like that, novelists

48
00:04:38,136 --> 00:04:41,699
who wrote about the Orient, you know, like Disraeli or Flaubert.

49
00:04:42,199 --> 00:04:45,942
And, you know, the fact that those representations of the Orient had very little to do with

50
00:04:46,002 --> 00:04:49,104
what I knew about my own background in life.

51
00:04:49,885 --> 00:04:51,486
So I decided to write the history of that.

52
00:05:05,748 --> 00:05:08,849
If somebody, let's say in the 1850s or 60s in

53
00:05:09,569 --> 00:05:10,469
Paris or London,

54
00:05:11,510 --> 00:05:13,030
wished to talk about

55
00:05:13,810 --> 00:05:14,510
or read about

56
00:05:15,671 --> 00:05:15,971
India

57
00:05:16,811 --> 00:05:18,551
or Egypt or Syria,

58
00:05:19,792 --> 00:05:22,853
there would be very little chance for that person to

59
00:05:24,013 --> 00:05:24,493
simply

60
00:05:26,314 --> 00:05:27,314
address the subject,

61
00:05:29,294 --> 00:05:31,935
as we like to think, in a kind of free and creative way.

62
00:05:33,155 --> 00:05:34,936
A great deal of writing had gone before.

63
00:05:36,850 --> 00:05:43,696
And this writing was an organized form of writing, like an organized science, what I've

64
00:05:43,736 --> 00:05:44,657
called Orientalism.

65
00:05:45,357 --> 00:05:46,218
And it seemed to me that

66
00:05:47,059 --> 00:05:51,082
there was a kind of repertory of images that kept coming up.

67
00:05:51,583 --> 00:05:56,096
The sensual woman. who's there to be sort of used by the man.

68
00:05:57,037 --> 00:06:01,399
The East is a kind of mysterious place full of secrets and monsters.

69
00:06:01,819 --> 00:06:04,360
You know, the marvels of the East was a phrase that was used.

70
00:06:04,921 --> 00:06:08,602
And the more I looked, the more I saw that this was really quite consistent with itself.

71
00:06:09,143 --> 00:06:10,603
You know, it had very little to do with

72
00:06:11,724 --> 00:06:12,824
people who'd actually been there.

73
00:06:13,785 --> 00:06:16,066
And even if they had been there, there wasn't much modification.

74
00:06:16,086 --> 00:06:20,088
In other words, you didn't get what you could call realistic representations of the Orient,

75
00:06:20,188 --> 00:06:22,129
either in literature or in painting or in music

76
00:06:23,949 --> 00:06:28,055
any of the arts. And this extended even further into descriptions of the Arabs by

77
00:06:29,277 --> 00:06:31,020
experts, you know, people who had

78
00:06:32,121 --> 00:06:32,642
studied them.

79
00:06:33,648 --> 00:06:37,510
And I noticed that even in the 20th century, some of the same images that you found in the

80
00:06:37,530 --> 00:06:39,431
19th century

81
00:06:40,331 --> 00:06:45,354
amongst scholars like Edward William Lane, who wrote his book on the modern Egyptians in the

82
00:06:45,434 --> 00:06:50,837
early 1830s, and then you read somebody in the 1920s, and they're more or less saying the same

83
00:06:50,877 --> 00:06:51,117
thing.

84
00:06:52,177 --> 00:06:57,780
One great example that I always give is that the wonderful French poet, Gérard de Nerval, who

85
00:06:57,820 --> 00:07:00,561
went on a voyage to the Orient, as he called it.

86
00:07:00,681 --> 00:07:04,523
And I was reading this book of his travels in Syria, and there was something very familiar

87
00:07:04,563 --> 00:07:07,244
about it. You know, it sounded like something else that I'd read.

88
00:07:07,924 --> 00:07:11,365
And then I realized that what he was doing almost unconsciously was quoting Lane

89
00:07:12,085 --> 00:07:15,947
on the Egyptians, on the theory that the Orientals are all the same no matter where you find

90
00:07:15,987 --> 00:07:18,848
them. I mean, it's in India or in Syria or in Egypt.

91
00:07:19,145 --> 00:07:20,386
It's basically the same essence.

92
00:07:20,806 --> 00:07:24,529
So it develops a kind of image of the timeless Orient, as if the Orient,

93
00:07:25,230 --> 00:07:26,570
unlike the West, doesn't develop.

94
00:07:26,631 --> 00:07:29,413
It stays the same. And that's one of the problems with Orientalism is

95
00:07:30,233 --> 00:07:30,874
it creates

96
00:07:32,115 --> 00:07:36,678
an image outside of history of something that is placid and still and

97
00:07:37,799 --> 00:07:38,239
eternal,

98
00:07:39,240 --> 00:07:41,983
which is simply contradicted by the fact of history.

99
00:07:42,603 --> 00:07:46,086
So in that one sense, it's a creation

100
00:07:46,987 --> 00:07:49,029
of, you might say, an ideal other

101
00:07:50,009 --> 00:07:50,510
for Europe.

102
00:08:03,047 --> 00:08:08,231
Professor Said's analysis of Orientalism isn't just a description of its content, but a sustained

103
00:08:08,352 --> 00:08:10,313
argument for why it looks the way it does.

104
00:08:11,374 --> 00:08:16,559
It's an examination of the quite concrete historical and institutional context that creates

105
00:08:16,619 --> 00:08:16,699
it.

106
00:08:18,220 --> 00:08:23,905
Specifically, Said locates the construction of Orientalism within the history of imperial conquest.

107
00:08:25,315 --> 00:08:26,896
As empires spread across the globe,

108
00:08:27,636 --> 00:08:31,257
historically the British and the French have been the most important in terms of the East.

109
00:08:32,017 --> 00:08:36,239
They conquer not only militarily, but also what we could call ideologically.

110
00:08:37,719 --> 00:08:39,160
The question for these empires is,

111
00:08:39,880 --> 00:08:44,321
how do we understand the natives that we are encountering so we can conquer and subdue them

112
00:08:44,462 --> 00:08:44,782
easier?

113
00:08:46,362 --> 00:08:51,124
This process of using large abstract categories to explain people who look different, whose

114
00:08:51,164 --> 00:08:54,005
skin is a different color, has been going on for a long time.

115
00:08:54,913 --> 00:08:57,934
as far back as there has been contact between different cultures and peoples.

116
00:08:58,674 --> 00:09:04,136
But Orientalism makes this general process more formal in that it presents itself as objective

117
00:09:04,216 --> 00:09:04,556
knowledge.

118
00:09:06,297 --> 00:09:12,459
Said identifies Napoleon's conquest of Egypt in 1798 as marking a new kind of imperial and

119
00:09:12,499 --> 00:09:16,360
colonial conquest that inaugurates the project of Orientalism.

120
00:09:16,940 --> 00:09:18,021
There was a kind of break

121
00:09:18,761 --> 00:09:20,982
that occurred after Napoleon came to Egypt in 1798.

122
00:09:23,435 --> 00:09:26,040
I think it's the first really important

123
00:09:26,961 --> 00:09:27,823
modern imperial

124
00:09:28,865 --> 00:09:29,486
expedition.

125
00:09:30,467 --> 00:09:35,055
So he invades the place, but he doesn't invade it the way the Spaniards invaded the New World.

126
00:09:35,937 --> 00:09:42,183
looking for loot. He comes instead with an enormous army of soldiers, but also scientists,

127
00:09:43,043 --> 00:09:45,766
botanists, architects, philologists,

128
00:09:46,747 --> 00:09:48,709
biologists, historians,

129
00:09:49,629 --> 00:09:52,132
whose job it was to record Egypt

130
00:09:53,073 --> 00:09:54,294
in every conceivable way

131
00:09:55,355 --> 00:10:00,239
and produce a kind of scientific survey of Egypt, which was designed not for the Egyptian,

132
00:10:00,800 --> 00:10:01,700
but for the European.

133
00:10:02,925 --> 00:10:06,747
And, of course, what strikes you, first of all, about the volumes that they produced is their

134
00:10:06,787 --> 00:10:09,068
enormous size. They're a meter square.

135
00:10:10,588 --> 00:10:16,591
And all across them is written the power and prestige of a modern European country that can

136
00:10:16,671 --> 00:10:17,732
do to the Egyptians

137
00:10:18,512 --> 00:10:20,353
what the Egyptians cannot do to the French.

138
00:10:20,393 --> 00:10:23,174
I mean, there's no comparable Egyptian survey of France.

139
00:10:23,834 --> 00:10:26,455
To produce knowledge, you have to have a power

140
00:10:27,216 --> 00:10:27,756
to be there,

141
00:10:28,765 --> 00:10:33,286
and to see in expert ways things that the natives themselves can't see.

142
00:10:45,088 --> 00:10:45,968
The differences between

143
00:10:46,908 --> 00:10:48,348
different kinds of Orientalisms

144
00:10:50,049 --> 00:10:52,589
are, in effect, the differences between different experiences of

145
00:10:53,369 --> 00:10:54,449
what is called the Orient.

146
00:10:54,869 --> 00:10:55,910
I mean, the difference between

147
00:10:56,630 --> 00:10:59,836
Britain and France on the one hand and the united states on the other

148
00:11:00,677 --> 00:11:07,021
is that britain and france had colonies in the orient i mean they had a long-standing uh relationship

149
00:11:07,501 --> 00:11:07,641
and

150
00:11:08,682 --> 00:11:15,527
imperial uh role in a place like india yeah so that there's a kind of a uh

151
00:11:15,567 --> 00:11:17,228
there's a kind of a archive

152
00:11:18,128 --> 00:11:19,469
of actual experiences

153
00:11:20,330 --> 00:11:24,913
of being in india of ruling the country for several hundred years right

154
00:11:26,563 --> 00:11:32,512
And the same with the French in North Africa, let's say Algeria or Indochina, direct colonial

155
00:11:32,572 --> 00:11:35,857
experience. In the case of the Americans, the experience is

156
00:11:36,698 --> 00:11:37,940
much less direct.

157
00:11:38,952 --> 00:11:44,158
there's never been an american occupation of the near east so i would say the difference between

158
00:11:44,178 --> 00:11:45,919
british and french orientalism on the one hand

159
00:11:46,700 --> 00:11:51,105
and the american experience of the orient on the other is that the american one is much more

160
00:11:51,125 --> 00:11:52,606
um

161
00:11:53,567 --> 00:11:56,811
indirect it's much more based on abstractions

162
00:11:57,772 --> 00:11:58,232
the second

163
00:11:59,654 --> 00:12:05,078
I think the difference in the American experience from the British and the French of Orientalism

164
00:12:05,679 --> 00:12:08,702
is that American Orientalism is very politicized

165
00:12:09,562 --> 00:12:10,043
by the

166
00:12:11,744 --> 00:12:12,625
presence of Israel,

167
00:12:13,386 --> 00:12:15,748
for which America is the main ally.

168
00:12:16,295 --> 00:12:22,039
President Clinton and I are proud, as are all Americans, that the United States was the first

169
00:12:22,099 --> 00:12:29,024
nation to recognize the state of Israel, 11 minutes after you proclaimed your independence.

170
00:12:30,405 --> 00:12:34,707
And what you have, in effect, is the creation of a Jewish state in the middle of the Islamic

171
00:12:35,048 --> 00:12:35,788
Oriental world,

172
00:12:36,849 --> 00:12:40,872
and the sense that because it's a Jewish state and a Western state,

173
00:12:41,672 --> 00:12:42,373
self-declared,

174
00:12:43,464 --> 00:12:46,448
There is a greater coincidence between American interests

175
00:12:47,189 --> 00:12:52,475
there than there is between American interests, let's say, in places like Iran and Saudi Arabia,

176
00:12:52,495 --> 00:12:53,796
which are important because of oil.

177
00:12:54,617 --> 00:12:55,819
I think the presence

178
00:12:56,579 --> 00:12:59,203
of this other factor, which is very anti-Islamic,

179
00:12:59,923 --> 00:13:03,808
where Israel regards the whole Arab world as its enemy, is imported into

180
00:13:05,052 --> 00:13:06,673
into American Orientalism.

181
00:13:06,873 --> 00:13:11,296
I mean, the idea, for example, that Hamas terrorists on the West Bank are just interested in

182
00:13:11,336 --> 00:13:12,317
killing Jewish children

183
00:13:13,318 --> 00:13:15,779
is what you derive from looking at this stuff.

184
00:13:15,819 --> 00:13:19,162
And very little attention is paid to the fact that the Israeli occupation of the West Bank

185
00:13:19,202 --> 00:13:21,603
and Gaza has been going on for 30 years.

186
00:13:21,643 --> 00:13:23,885
It's the longest military occupation in this century.

187
00:13:25,059 --> 00:13:29,384
And so you get the impression that the only problem is that Israeli security is threatened

188
00:13:29,404 --> 00:13:32,408
by Hamas and suicide bombs and all the rest of it.

189
00:13:32,548 --> 00:13:37,735
And nothing is said about the hundreds of thousands, millions of Palestinians who are dispossessed

190
00:13:37,775 --> 00:13:40,999
and living miserable lives as the direct result of what Israel has done and is doing.

191
00:13:41,199 --> 00:13:43,082
So there's a sense in which the Arab

192
00:13:44,412 --> 00:13:49,034
struggle for national independence and, in the case of the Palestinians, for national self-determination

193
00:13:49,094 --> 00:13:53,315
is looked at with great hostility as upsetting the stability of the status quo.

194
00:13:53,615 --> 00:13:53,955
And that

195
00:13:54,995 --> 00:13:59,057
makes it virtually impossible, it's a tragedy, virtually impossible for an

196
00:14:00,157 --> 00:14:02,938
American to see on television, to read books,

197
00:14:04,510 --> 00:14:09,292
to see films about the Middle East that are not colored politically by this conflict in which

198
00:14:09,332 --> 00:14:11,893
the Arabs almost always play the role of terrorists and

199
00:14:12,833 --> 00:14:13,353
violent

200
00:14:14,153 --> 00:14:16,034
people and irrational and so on and

201
00:14:16,954 --> 00:14:20,156
so

202
00:14:21,116 --> 00:14:31,460
forth.

203
00:14:31,560 --> 00:14:32,340
That's another thing.

204
00:14:33,541 --> 00:14:38,702
that America really needs to think about is our racism, racism that comes from the United States

205
00:14:38,742 --> 00:14:41,343
towards Muslim people and towards Arabic people.

206
00:14:41,683 --> 00:14:42,943
And that's something that has to stop.

207
00:14:43,043 --> 00:14:47,584
And the United States has to start respecting people from the Middle East in order to find

208
00:14:47,624 --> 00:14:48,245
a solution

209
00:14:48,965 --> 00:14:51,265
to the problem that's been building up over many years.

210
00:14:52,246 --> 00:14:56,747
So I thank everyone for your patience in letting me speak my mind on that.

211
00:14:57,787 --> 00:15:02,168
Many people believe the way that Americans understand the Muslim world is very problematic.

212
00:15:03,423 --> 00:15:06,825
Indeed, anti-Arab racism seems to be almost officially sanctioned.

213
00:15:07,586 --> 00:15:12,129
You can make generalized and racist statements about Arab peoples that would not be tolerated

214
00:15:12,169 --> 00:15:13,030
for any other group.

215
00:15:14,291 --> 00:15:19,354
At the heart of how this new American Orientalism operates is a threatening and demonized figure

216
00:15:19,814 --> 00:15:23,417
of the Islamic terrorist that is emphasized by journalists and Hollywood.

217
00:15:25,091 --> 00:15:30,059
Now, Said recognizes that terrorism exists as a result of the violent political situation in

218
00:15:30,079 --> 00:15:34,807
the Middle East. But he argues that there is a lot more going on there that is misunderstood

219
00:15:34,988 --> 00:15:36,711
or not seen by the peoples of the West.

220
00:15:38,183 --> 00:15:43,149
The result of the media's focus on one negative aspect alone means that all the peoples of

221
00:15:43,189 --> 00:15:47,134
the Islamic world come to be understood in the same negative and paranoid way.

222
00:15:47,714 --> 00:15:52,220
That is as a threat. So that when we think of people who look like that and who come from that

223
00:15:52,260 --> 00:15:54,803
part of the world, we think fanatic,

224
00:15:55,744 --> 00:15:56,224
extreme,

225
00:15:57,105 --> 00:15:57,486
violent.

226
00:15:59,053 --> 00:16:03,834
Said argues that understanding a vast and complex region like the Middle East in this narrow

227
00:16:03,874 --> 00:16:09,216
way takes away from the humanity and diversity of millions of ordinary people living decent

228
00:16:09,316 --> 00:16:15,418
and humane lives there. We asked, would he plant a bomb to blow up the Americans if the Islamic

229
00:16:15,578 --> 00:16:17,078
underground asked him to?

230
00:16:17,758 --> 00:16:22,840
The answer was yes. After I'd written Orientalism and a book called The Question of Palestine

231
00:16:22,860 --> 00:16:25,720
in the early 80s, in the late 70s rather, and

232
00:16:26,441 --> 00:16:27,901
beginning of the 80s, I wrote a third book.

233
00:16:28,487 --> 00:16:31,249
which is called Covering Islam, and I thought of them as a kind of trilogy.

234
00:16:31,949 --> 00:16:34,650
And Covering Islam was an account of the

235
00:16:36,431 --> 00:16:38,192
coverage of Islam in the popular media,

236
00:16:39,213 --> 00:16:45,396
immediately occasioned by the Iranian Revolution, which described itself, as you recall, as

237
00:16:45,576 --> 00:16:46,657
an Islamic revolution.

238
00:16:47,578 --> 00:16:51,040
And what I discovered was a huge

239
00:16:54,061 --> 00:16:54,501
arsenal

240
00:16:55,442 --> 00:16:57,123
of images employed by the media.

241
00:16:58,304 --> 00:17:01,285
