00:32:57,951 --> 00:33:03,013
we live apart in fear and loathing of each other, constantly under threat, constantly at war.

485
00:33:04,422 --> 00:33:07,083
In seeking a way out of this legacy of mistrust and conflict,

486
00:33:07,803 --> 00:33:13,185
Said draws upon the work of Italian philosopher Antonio Gramsci, who gives us the tools to

487
00:33:13,245 --> 00:33:16,707
think about these difficult issues in more productive and humane ways.

488
00:33:18,027 --> 00:33:22,849
Well, Gramsci, in the prison notebooks, says something that has always

489
00:33:24,230 --> 00:33:26,130
tremendously appealed to me, that history

490
00:33:27,311 --> 00:33:28,491
deposits in us

491
00:33:29,211 --> 00:33:33,093
our own history, our family's history, our nation's history, our tradition's history.

492
00:33:33,852 --> 00:33:37,574
which has left in us an infinity of traces, all kinds of

493
00:33:38,314 --> 00:33:43,817
marks, through heredity, through collective experience, through individual experience, through

494
00:33:43,877 --> 00:33:45,778
family experience, through relations between

495
00:33:46,699 --> 00:33:47,699
one individual and another.

496
00:33:47,719 --> 00:33:47,739
A

497
00:33:49,100 --> 00:33:49,540
whole

498
00:33:51,861 --> 00:33:52,662
book, if you like,

499
00:33:53,602 --> 00:33:56,164
a series of an infinity of traces.

500
00:33:57,044 --> 00:33:58,305
But there's no inventory.

501
00:33:58,365 --> 00:34:00,586
There's no orderly guide to it.

502
00:34:02,275 --> 00:34:07,838
So Gramsci says, therefore the task at the outset is to try to compile an inventory.

503
00:34:07,978 --> 00:34:09,799
In other words, to try and make sense of it.

504
00:34:10,880 --> 00:34:15,662
And this seems to me, at any rate, to be the most interesting sort of human task.

505
00:34:16,783 --> 00:34:18,203
It's the task of interpretation.

506
00:34:18,964 --> 00:34:21,525
It's the task of giving history some shape and sense.

507
00:34:23,167 --> 00:34:27,530
for a particular reason, not just to show that my history is better than yours, or my history

508
00:34:27,550 --> 00:34:31,372
is worse than yours. I'm a victim, and you're somebody who's oppressed people.

509
00:34:31,873 --> 00:34:35,955
But rather, to understand my history in terms of other people's history.

510
00:34:35,975 --> 00:34:39,958
In other words, to try to understand, to move beyond, to generalize

511
00:34:40,698 --> 00:34:42,820
one's own individual experience to the experience of others.

512
00:34:44,103 --> 00:34:46,584
And I think the great

513
00:34:49,165 --> 00:34:49,585
goal

514
00:34:50,365 --> 00:34:53,426
is, in fact, to become someone else, to transform itself

515
00:34:54,567 --> 00:34:57,368
from a unitary identity to an identity that

516
00:34:58,168 --> 00:35:01,369
includes the other without suppressing the difference.

517
00:35:01,829 --> 00:35:05,210
That, he says, is the great goal.

518
00:35:05,610 --> 00:35:08,491
And for me, I think that would be the case.

519
00:35:09,292 --> 00:35:09,872
And that would be the

520
00:35:10,752 --> 00:35:11,592
notion of writing

521
00:35:12,573 --> 00:35:13,253
an inventory,

522
00:35:14,313 --> 00:35:17,896
historical inventory. We try not only to understand oneself but to understand oneself

523
00:35:18,636 --> 00:35:22,018
in relation to others and to understand others as if you would understand yourself.

524
00:35:22,979 --> 00:35:25,100
Palestine is so important in this respect

525
00:35:26,581 --> 00:35:27,101
because of its

526
00:35:28,002 --> 00:35:28,422
local

527
00:35:29,523 --> 00:35:31,744
complexities, let's say Arabs and Jews,

528
00:35:32,985 --> 00:35:38,088
Arab Muslims and Arab Christians and Israeli Jews of themselves very mixed backgrounds.

529
00:35:38,148 --> 00:35:41,150
I mean we're talking about Polish Jews, Russian Jews, American Jews,

530
00:35:41,967 --> 00:35:44,148
Yemeni Jews, Iraqi Jews, Indian Jews.

531
00:35:44,528 --> 00:35:49,449
It's a fairly complex mosaic, somehow finding a way to live together

532
00:35:50,910 --> 00:35:51,830
on a land that is

533
00:35:52,750 --> 00:35:55,231
drenched, saturated with

534
00:35:56,391 --> 00:35:59,792
significance on a world scale, unlike any other country in the world.

535
00:35:59,952 --> 00:36:02,813
I mean, it's holy to three of the major religions,

536
00:36:03,773 --> 00:36:08,535
and every inch of it has been combed over and fought over for the last several thousand years.

537
00:36:09,811 --> 00:36:15,376
And the pattern so far has been the Zionist pattern, which is to say that it was promised to

538
00:36:15,516 --> 00:36:16,797
us. We're the chosen people.

539
00:36:17,718 --> 00:36:19,179
Everybody else is sort of second rate.

540
00:36:19,279 --> 00:36:22,081
Throw them out or treat them as second class citizens.

541
00:36:23,302 --> 00:36:23,442
And

542
00:36:24,463 --> 00:36:25,644
in contrast to that,

543
00:36:26,385 --> 00:36:29,507
some of us, not everybody, but many Palestinians have said, well,

544
00:36:30,408 --> 00:36:34,832
we realize that we are being asked to pay the price for what happened to the Jews in Europe

545
00:36:35,452 --> 00:36:38,695
under the Holocaust. It was an entirely Christian and European

546
00:36:39,943 --> 00:36:42,585
catastrophe in which the Arabs played no part.

547
00:36:44,267 --> 00:36:44,447
And

548
00:36:45,167 --> 00:36:47,109
we are being dispossessed, displaced

549
00:36:48,530 --> 00:36:50,912
by the victims. We've become the victims of the victims.

550
00:36:51,312 --> 00:36:55,015
But as I say, not all of us say, well, they should be thrown out,

551
00:36:56,016 --> 00:36:57,497
because we have been thrown out.

552
00:36:57,557 --> 00:37:00,180
And so we have another vision, which is a vision of coexistence.

553
00:37:01,063 --> 00:37:03,524
in which Jew and Arab, Muslim, Christian,

554
00:37:04,705 --> 00:37:05,085
and Jew,

555
00:37:05,905 --> 00:37:11,308
can live together in some polity, which I think it requires a kind of creativity and invention

556
00:37:12,969 --> 00:37:14,169
that is possible, vision,

557
00:37:15,310 --> 00:37:16,110
that would replace

558
00:37:17,311 --> 00:37:19,472
the authoritarian hierarchical model.

559
00:37:20,132 --> 00:37:24,815
But this idea that somehow we should protect ourselves against the infiltrations, the infections

560
00:37:24,875 --> 00:37:25,315
of the other,

561
00:37:26,585 --> 00:37:28,747
is, I think, the most dangerous idea

562
00:37:29,768 --> 00:37:31,550
at the end of the 20th century.

563
00:37:31,670 --> 00:37:31,810
And

564
00:37:32,831 --> 00:37:36,194
unless we find ways to do it, and there are no shortcuts to it,

565
00:37:38,296 --> 00:37:41,379
unless we find ways to do this, there's going to be wholesale

566
00:37:42,400 --> 00:37:43,361
violence of the sort

567
00:37:44,762 --> 00:37:46,384
represented by the Gulf War, by the

568
00:37:47,185 --> 00:37:50,468
killings in Bosnia, the Rwandan massacres, and so on.

569
00:37:50,508 --> 00:37:51,388
I mean, those are the pattern.

570
00:37:52,607 --> 00:37:56,449
of emerging conflict that is extremely dangerous and needs to be counteracted.

571
00:37:56,649 --> 00:37:59,430
And I think therefore it's correct to say that the challenge now

572
00:38:00,150 --> 00:38:01,911
is the challenge, I wouldn't call it

573
00:38:04,212 --> 00:38:05,572
anything other than coexistence.

574
00:38:06,032 --> 00:38:09,794
How does one coexist with people whose religions are different, whose

575
00:38:11,034 --> 00:38:15,376
traditions and languages are different, but who form part of the same community or polity

576
00:38:16,416 --> 00:38:17,317
in a national sense?

577
00:38:18,658 --> 00:38:22,661
How do we accept difference without violence and hostility?

578
00:38:24,642 --> 00:38:28,785
I've been interested in a field called comparative literature most all of my adult life and

579
00:38:29,265 --> 00:38:31,006
the ideal of comparative literature is not

580
00:38:31,887 --> 00:38:36,110
to show how English literature is really a secondary phenomenon in French literature or Arabic

581
00:38:36,150 --> 00:38:36,510
literature

582
00:38:37,990 --> 00:38:41,571
you know, a kind of poor cousin to Persian literature or any of those silly things, but to

583
00:38:41,611 --> 00:38:43,792
show them existing, you might say, as

584
00:38:44,553 --> 00:38:45,753
contrapuntal lines

585
00:38:46,533 --> 00:38:48,634
in a great composition by which difference

586
00:38:49,935 --> 00:38:51,115
is respected and understood

587
00:38:53,416 --> 00:38:54,337
without coercion.

588
00:38:55,957 --> 00:38:57,558
And it's that attitude, I think, that we need.
